WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.630
So, when you start using Claude Code,

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:03.510
you actually have the option for three

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:05.870
surfaces by which you can use it in. You

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.270
have the Claude desktop app, you have

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:09.670
your Claude Code extension for an IDE

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:11.270
software, basically an integrated

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.070
development environment, and lastly, you

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.110
can use Claude Code in the terminal. So,

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.470
in this module, I'll differentiate

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.150
between those three so that you can

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.310
decide which one to use. And for those

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:21.910
three surfaces, remember that you can

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:24.790
just go to claude.ai/downloads

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.790
and just scroll down in here, and if you

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:28.550
find Claude Code, you'll be able to

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.510
install the version for the terminal.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:32.269
You can install the one for VS Code,

00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:34.270
which is going to be your IDE software,

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.110
and last but not least is the desktop

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.550
app, which you can download here. And no

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.190
matter which surface you choose, just

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:40.590
keep in mind that under the hood, it's

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.830
going to be the same models, Opus,

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:44.710
Sonnet, and Haiku, which are Anthropic's

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:46.430
tiered models from the strongest to the

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:48.070
weakest, respectively. And it's going to

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.030
be the same harness, which is basically

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.390
the set of code and system prompts that

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.310
Anthropic has wrapped those models

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.070
around to give you the capabilities of

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:57.550
Claude Code. So, when it comes to the

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.670
desktop app, you can see here that it is

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:01.710
basically going to be similar to what

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.350
you may be used to with something like

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.710
ChatGPT or Gemini or even the chat

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:08.030
window in claude.ai. But, if you look at

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.750
the top left, there's actually three

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.830
tabs in here. The first one is chat,

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:14.070
which basically explains things for you.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:15.910
So, amongst these three, it is the least

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:18.110
capable, and I often find that I don't

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.430
really use chat that much because Claude

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.230
Work as well as Claude Code can pretty

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.590
much do whatever Claude Chat does. Now,

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.230
the second level up is going to be

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:28.310
Claude Work, which you may have seen in

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.310
several tutorials before, but in my

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.110
view, it is more of a watered-down

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:33.750
version of Claude Code. And what you

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:35.430
need to keep in mind is that everything

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:37.230
that you can do in Claude Work, you can

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.310
actually use and do in Claude Code. And

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:40.510
so, if you're trying to master these

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:43.070
agentic tools and also use and be at the

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:44.870
bleeding edge of these technologies,

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.750
then I recommend for you to start with

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:48.510
Claude Code, which is the third level

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.630
here and is also the most powerful. And

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.270
at the end of the day, it is actually

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:53.750
quite similar to chat anyway, where you

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.350
have a chat window in here. And even

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.630
though the name Claude Code has code in

00:01:57.640 --> 00:01:59.470
that, that's more a function of the fact

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:01.630
that it began as a software development

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.430
tool, but slowly but surely, because of

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:05.670
its raw capability to automate so much

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:07.550
more versus just software development,

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:08.749
then Cloud Code actually has the

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:10.910
potential to be used by much more than

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:12.990
just software development nowadays. And

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.550
so, in the desktop app, if I were to

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:16.710
sort of encapsulate it, if you want the

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:19.350
simplest experience, as well as the one

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.150
that is least intimidating, then using

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:22.910
Cloud Code via the desktop app might

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.949
actually be good for you to start with.

00:02:24.959 --> 00:02:26.110
There are some limitations to it, which

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:27.630
I will show later when we go to the

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:29.750
other more flexible surfaces of using

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.750
Cloud Code, but in essence, to use Cloud

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.150
Code anywhere, the first thing that you

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:35.510
need to do is to select a workspace,

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:37.630
which is basically just a folder in your

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:39.270
laptop. And how you can do that is just

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.070
click on this folder icon, and you can

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:42.949
see here I have a couple of workspaces

00:02:42.959 --> 00:02:44.510
in here. And what you just need to do is

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.230
to open a folder, and in selecting your

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.390
workspace, this can be really any folder

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.590
on your device. So, for example, you can

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:52.790
see here I have this empty folder that

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.550
is named My Workspace. I can just go

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:56.630
ahead and select that. And if I click on

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.030
Trust Workspace, what that basically

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:00.949
does is it allows Cloud Code to look at

00:03:00.959 --> 00:03:02.949
this folder and give it that permission

00:03:02.959 --> 00:03:05.550
to write files or create files within

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:06.830
this folder. So, just to give you an

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.430
example, let's say we want a Hello World

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:11.910
text file in this workspace, then that

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:13.550
would just command your Cloud Code to

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.870
create that file within your device. And

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:17.509
this is going to be important later,

00:03:17.519 --> 00:03:19.150
because once you start automating

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:20.790
things, and once you start creating

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.310
files in mass across multiple sessions,

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:25.350
then this is going to be really powerful

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:26.990
for you. Because at least for this one

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:28.870
test, we only created one text file, but

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:30.710
imagine once you can create multiple,

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.190
let's say, PowerPoint presentations,

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.390
multiple videos, multiple images, by

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:36.030
sort of fine-tuning your Cloud Code

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:37.830
within this one workspace, then you'll

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:39.910
be able to arrive at a point where this

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:41.910
particular folder that you just assigned

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:43.630
is going to be sort of your operating

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:45.870
system or second brain, is what some

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:47.430
people call it, that you can just query

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:49.470
or ask. So, that is what we will unpack

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:51.710
as we go forward with this course. To

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:53.870
give one example from my workspace,

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:56.150
you'll actually see me use this Robo

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:58.110
folder a lot. And you can see here that

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:00.870
I have several files and folders already

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:02.430
in here. There are some images and

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:04.110
videos stored in this folder. There are

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:05.670
several markdown files, which are

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:07.270
basically text files that are also

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.310
stored in this this workspace. And the

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:10.990
mind you, all of these files, which I

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:13.710
think is collectively something like 8

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:16.789
GB in size already, is all created by

00:04:16.799 --> 00:04:18.710
CloudCode. So, whenever I do any sort of

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:20.550
knowledge work now, I just operate

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.590
within just this one workspace. And I

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:24.230
personally don't even have it memorized

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:25.830
what files are in here, because the

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:27.270
models that we are using now, for

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:29.950
example, Opus 4.8 or Sonnet even, they

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:31.909
can parse through that file list, that

00:04:31.919 --> 00:04:33.830
whole workspace, at almost lightning

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.430
speed. So, even if you have thousands of

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:36.710
files in there, it's going to be no

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:38.470
sweat for them to parse through the

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:40.230
whole thing. Anyway, that sort of design

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:41.870
for the files and folders is going to be

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.070
a topic for a future module. But for

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:45.790
now, I just wanted to illustrate that at

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:47.110
least for the CloudCode desktop app, it

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:48.950
is very basic. You have a chat window in

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.350
here. You can create files from within

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:53.150
your said workspace. And in fact, you

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:55.470
can open multiple folders or workspaces

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:56.790
in here. And if you click on this

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:58.710
filter, you can see I have my project

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:00.950
set to Robo, but let's say I want to

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:02.710
bring back that my workspace. You can

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:04.750
see here that chat session, as what we

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:06.710
call it, is included here. And if I need

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:08.350
to start a new one, then I can just

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:10.870
click on plus. Say this is session two

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:13.830
of my workspace, and that will appear on

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:15.630
the left as well. So, all of your

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:17.510
session chats will be included here.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:18.990
Now, before I go and show the other

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:21.030
surfaces for CloudCode, there's actually

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:22.350
two more things that I wanted just

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:23.950
showing the desktop app. One is very

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:25.550
soon you'll be uh running multiple

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:27.630
sessions in just one screen, and that

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:29.470
will just 10x your productivity. And

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:30.790
what I mean by that is, let's say you

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:32.590
have this chat window, and let's say you

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:35.190
assign it to do a deep research task on,

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:37.550
for example, marketing techniques for

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:39.270
real estate. Let's say that is sort of

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:40.630
the industry that you are in. You will

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:42.430
find that CloudCode, because of its

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:44.590
agentic nature, it will work for a few

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:46.590
minutes before giving you the final

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:48.750
output. And that is just natural. It is

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:50.630
just the way by which these agents are

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:52.350
actually doing things. By default, you

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:54.310
can see that it asks you clarifying

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:56.390
questions because that turns out to be a

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.150
best practice so that you are fully

00:05:58.160 --> 00:05:59.950
aligned with your agent in terms of what

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:01.830
it is that you want as a final output.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:03.790
And once it's clear, it is basically

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:06.030
going to start this process of a few

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:07.950
minutes of doing that deep research. And

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:09.350
obviously, what you would want is to

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:10.910
start another session so that you can do

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:12.870
something else while this agent is

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:14.230
currently working. So, at least for the

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:16.270
Claude desktop app, one essential skill

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:17.550
that would be good for you to understand

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:19.310
right now is you can actually open a new

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:20.710
session in here. Let's say this is going

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:22.350
to be our session three. And if you go

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:23.909
and highlight this workspace session two

00:06:23.919 --> 00:06:25.790
and click on this ellipsis, if you click

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:28.150
on open in split view, you'll actually

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:29.710
be able to see this other session

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:31.590
happening concurrently on the side. And

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:33.870
you can do this up to four times, which

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:36.390
is pretty nifty. So, for example, if I

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:38.590
open this third one, then you'll be able

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:40.630
to see that open on the third side

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:42.390
there. If I click on plus once again,

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:43.750
and let's say this is going to be our

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:45.830
session four, then if I do the same

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:47.470
thing for this last session here, which

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:49.030
you can see is not highlighted, and I

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:50.790
click open in split view, then you can

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:52.870
start to see how a lot of power users,

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:54.430
including myself in the VS Code

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:56.150
extension, which I'll show later, can

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:58.550
actually run multiple concurrent agents.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:00.310
So, you can give a task to this agent,

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:01.750
you can give a task to this agent, and

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:03.430
so on. And this is just a nice way for

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:05.390
you to view everything in one full

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:07.150
suite. But this is also actually part of

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:09.030
the reason why I'm not using the desktop

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:10.990
app personally as my daily driver. It's

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:13.110
mainly because if I want to, let's say,

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:15.790
readjust where these tabs are in so that

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:18.150
I can organize them better, it is quite

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:19.630
limited in its functionality at the

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:21.550
moment. I can only resize them, but I

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:23.270
can't actually drag them out, and I

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:26.070
can't actually reorder them to suit my

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:27.670
needs. The other thing that you should

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:29.190
know about the desktop app is that they

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:31.430
actually make it easy for you to look

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:33.710
for your routines, which are basically

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:35.670
scheduled tasks, which we'll tackle more

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:37.470
in the future lesson. And if you go to

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:39.510
this routines tab, you can see that out

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:41.790
of the box, Claude includes here a view

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:43.190
of the different routines that you can

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:45.070
set. You can pause or resume them if you

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:47.190
wish and even view them as a calendar.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:48.950
Only thing here is once you get to a

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:51.070
point where you have multiple routines,

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:52.590
usually it's actually much harder to

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:54.630
manage it with a user interface like

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:56.630
this, which you will see more as you use

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:57.990
Cloud Code more and more. The other

00:07:58.000 --> 00:07:59.550
thing that the Cloud desktop app ships

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:01.990
very nicely with is under this customize

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:04.630
view. Here you can see all of the skills

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:07.270
that your Cloud Code has access to. So

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:08.870
you can see right now I have this skill

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:10.790
creator skill that is mapped to Cloud

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:12.430
desktop. And then if you want to add a

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:14.310
skill manually, then you can just do

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:15.950
this here as well. Now I'll have to say

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:18.030
that for most Cloud Code users, because

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:19.990
this is sort of like an agent, like a an

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:21.750
actual digital employee that is living

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:23.790
in your computer, I never actually add

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:25.870
skills this way. Usually you just create

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:28.230
or add skills via natural language just

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:29.750
by talking to your Cloud Code, which we

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:31.230
will go through more in succeeding

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:33.630
modules. But if you need a nice list to

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:35.230
view all of your skills in one go, then

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:37.350
this is one place to see it. Same thing

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:39.149
with connectors or what we're calling as

00:08:39.159 --> 00:08:41.510
MCP connectors, you can see those here

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:43.790
as well. But similar to my sort of

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:45.870
warning earlier, once you really get

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:48.270
into using Cloud Code and you use it to

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:50.110
basically operate the work that you do,

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:52.550
then once you have like 90 plus skills

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:54.590
in here, which can be possible with some

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:56.190
setups, I think I personally have

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:57.990
something like 80 skills already, then

00:08:58.000 --> 00:08:59.950
there is almost no point to viewing them

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:02.230
listed here because if you need a skill

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:04.550
edited, it's so much more easier to just

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:06.710
ask Cloud Code to view that skill, to

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:08.710
edit it, and to just give it your

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:11.390
intention and have it handle the back

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:13.230
end tooling of it instead of having to

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:15.630
view your skills in here and actually

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:17.710
edit it yourself. So gone are the days

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:19.790
when you are editing files yourself,

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:22.230
gone are the days when you actually need

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:24.790
to super organize your files and

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:26.910
folders, that is basically the time of

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:29.630
previous software. Now with agents and

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:31.710
these AI models that are working at the

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:33.670
speed of light, then editing skill files

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:35.590
like these for example, it's no longer

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:37.590
up to you to type and edit, it's mostly

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:39.430
you dictating to the agent what you want

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:41.150
to happen, and it will do that for you

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:43.550
to edit these skill files. Now, that is

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:45.590
the desktop app, and that is actually a

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:47.750
sort of good way to frame things at the

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:49.310
base level because amongst the three

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:50.790
surfaces that I will show, I think the

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:52.910
desktop app is the weakest, but it is

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:54.830
the least intimidating. Now, generally,

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:56.030
even if you're a beginner, what I

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:57.630
recommend people to use in the very

00:09:57.640 --> 00:10:00.510
beginning is an IDE. And an IDE, in case

00:10:00.520 --> 00:10:01.710
you don't know, is an integrated

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:03.590
development environment. So, it is

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:05.790
exactly what it describes. It is an

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:07.270
environment, basically a piece of

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:09.910
software where you do development work,

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:11.990
and it is integrated because you can see

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:13.710
all of your files in there, and you can

00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:15.510
do all of your work in just one

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:17.510
software. The one that I advise people

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:19.870
to get is Visual Studio Code. It is open

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:22.790
source, been here for years already, and

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:24.350
you can just download it for free. That

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:26.230
is what I use personally, and you can

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:27.910
see I have Cloud Code in here. And just

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:29.510
to recap, in case you want Cloud Code

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:31.470
added to your VS Code, then you can just

00:10:31.480 --> 00:10:33.470
find it in the extensions in here, look

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:35.350
for Cloud Code for VS Code, and install

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:37.990
it. But, uh since this is an IDE, and it

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:39.430
is custom to you, you can actually

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:41.070
choose your own theme. You can move

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:42.670
things around in here. It's completely

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:43.950
up to you. But, the core working

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:46.310
principle of any IDE is that if you go

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:48.350
to the files in here, you can see we

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:50.350
have that same folder that we were

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:52.310
working on. You can see mine is Robo. If

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:53.950
you already have like a workspace that

00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:56.150
you are letting your Cloud Code operate

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:57.470
in, let's say you're migrating from the

00:10:57.480 --> 00:10:59.350
Cloud desktop app and have now decided

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:01.310
to use the IDE and the VS Code

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:02.910
extension, you can just do that as well.

00:11:02.920 --> 00:11:04.390
So, to give an example, I'll just open a

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:06.070
new window in here, and you can see this

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:08.390
is a new fresh instance, and I can just

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:10.990
open that folder, navigate to the folder

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:12.430
of choice of mine, which is my

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:13.870
workspace, for example, select that

00:11:13.880 --> 00:11:16.070
folder, and now you have VS Code

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:18.110
operating within that environment, that

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:19.829
folder. So, let's go ahead and trust the

00:11:19.839 --> 00:11:21.829
authors because the author is us. Now,

00:11:21.839 --> 00:11:23.510
just for avoidance of doubt, you can see

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:25.390
here that there is like this chat window

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:27.070
in here. That's just VS Code's attempt

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:29.710
to be a bit more agent capable as well.

00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:31.190
So, you don't really need that because

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:32.990
the one that we are using is Cloud Code.

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:34.430
And if you go here, you can see that you

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:36.150
have your list of sessions here at the

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:38.750
left. So, very familiar to you now if

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:40.030
you have used the Cloud Desktop app

00:11:40.040 --> 00:11:41.830
before. And if you start a new session,

00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:43.670
then that is literally going to open

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:45.510
just a chat window for you. And from

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:47.430
here, you can once again create files,

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:49.550
read files, have it go through your own

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:51.550
workspace to reorganize it. But for now,

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:52.950
to keep it simple, I'm asking it to

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:54.510
create a text file that says "Hello Robo

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:56.190
Nuggets." You can see it wrote that file

00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:58.190
for us, and it created that hello.text

00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:00.550
in our workspace. Now, what is unique

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:02.510
about VS Code, which I really like, is

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:04.270
that because it is an integrated

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:06.190
development environment, if I ask it to

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:07.870
open it here, then it will actually

00:12:07.880 --> 00:12:09.950
understand that and open that text file

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:11.910
right in VS Code's editor. So, this is

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:13.990
just a nice way to interact and preview

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:15.990
your files better, because from right

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:17.790
within VS Code, you can preview text

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:19.590
files, you can preview image files.

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:21.630
There are even plugins which Cloud Code

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:23.950
will recommend to you to preview PDFs.

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:25.150
And so, whatever knowledge work that

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:26.550
you're doing, you can pretty much do it

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:28.390
from within an IDE. The other thing that

00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:30.510
I really like about VS Code is, let's

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:33.230
say, you want this session that is doing

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:34.710
that deep research which we showed in

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:36.550
Cloud Desktop. If I start a new session

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:38.430
in here, that just opens a new window,

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:40.510
which if I drag to the left in here, it

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:42.829
is just much nicer to organize things.

00:12:42.839 --> 00:12:44.550
And if I start another session, then you

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:46.350
can just drag that as well, and you can

00:12:46.360 --> 00:12:48.350
just organize it however you see fit.

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:49.790
And so, at least for me, it's very

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:52.510
common to have multiple sessions from

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:54.510
right within this window that I just

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:56.710
sort of manage and switch between, so

00:12:56.720 --> 00:12:59.070
that I can do, let's say, 4x more work

00:12:59.080 --> 00:13:00.750
versus just chatting with just one

00:13:00.760 --> 00:13:02.430
window. And if you double click on this,

00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:04.390
because it is right now sort of taking

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:06.710
up majority of the screen, then that

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:08.630
will sort of equalize the width of them,

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:10.110
which is pretty nice, especially if you

00:13:10.120 --> 00:13:12.110
have like a second screen like I do,

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:14.350
where I sort of let these agents do

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:16.070
their work, and whenever they're done,

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:18.350
that is only when I step in to actually

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:19.829
review their work. And of course, the

00:13:19.839 --> 00:13:22.030
other benefit of the VS Code extension

00:13:22.040 --> 00:13:23.550
is that if you have your workspace open

00:13:23.560 --> 00:13:25.870
in here, this is pretty pretty that same

00:13:25.880 --> 00:13:27.470
folder that you were seeing earlier,

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:29.270
that Robo Workspace that I was talking

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:31.110
about, which sort of functions as our

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:32.950
operating system {slash} second brain

00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:34.950
now. And all of these folders are the

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:36.830
direct equivalents of these. Like, this

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:38.910
is literally it. And you can actually go

00:13:38.920 --> 00:13:41.270
through these and uh just look at the

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:43.510
contents of these markdown files from

00:13:43.520 --> 00:13:45.310
right within this IDE. So, if I just

00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:46.590
right-click and close the others there

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:48.510
so you can see, then you can preview

00:13:48.520 --> 00:13:49.750
like what we saw in the text file

00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:52.750
earlier, the contents of this file. Only

00:13:52.760 --> 00:13:54.430
sort of drawback with the VS Code

00:13:54.440 --> 00:13:56.070
extension is that you actually don't

00:13:56.080 --> 00:13:58.070
have an interface here to see your

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:00.270
skills in just one list. And uh the best

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:01.790
way that you can do that is to just

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:03.750
click on this {slash} icon. And if you

00:14:03.760 --> 00:14:05.230
look at the slash commands in here, that

00:14:05.240 --> 00:14:07.350
just shows some of the skills, or all of

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:09.190
the skills rather, that Cloud Code has

00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:10.710
access to. And you can see I have a lot

00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:12.030
in here. And so, this is what I mean

00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:13.990
when you start to use Cloud Code for

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:15.470
everything, for almost everything, then

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:17.190
you start to develop skills that you

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:18.950
don't really need to write yourself. If

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:20.550
in case you need to clean this up, for

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:22.430
example, which at least for me I already

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:24.150
have like 80 plus in here. There was

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:26.190
never really a need for me to clean it

00:14:26.200 --> 00:14:28.070
up, even at the level that I'm using it

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:29.470
in. So, that might be different for you,

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:30.950
but at least from my side, if in case I

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:32.270
do need to clean it up, then I'll just

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:34.510
ask Cloud Code to recommend to me what

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:35.790
are the ones that we can either

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:38.310
discontinue or delete or trim down, if

00:14:38.320 --> 00:14:40.630
there is ever a need. The other thing is

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:43.110
the MCP connectors, which uh let Cloud

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:44.710
Code connect to your tools. You can

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:46.829
actually also see them here under MCP

00:14:46.839 --> 00:14:48.430
servers. So, you can see I have a couple

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:49.910
of them in here, but uh in another

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:51.630
module we'll tackle the benefits of

00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:54.070
using an MCP versus other connector

00:14:54.080 --> 00:14:56.350
options. Now, last thing is the terminal

00:14:56.360 --> 00:14:57.910
view. Now, for most people I suggest to

00:14:57.920 --> 00:14:59.630
start with a VS Code extension because

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:01.070
the terminal view may be the most

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:03.070
intimidating amongst these three. So, to

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:04.910
access your terminal, you can just type

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:06.950
in your terminal app in here. That's

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:08.630
always been there, and if you're not a

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:09.990
developer, you may have not used it

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:11.430
before, and that's fine. But uh if you

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:13.110
click on that, it will open a window

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:14.670
similar to this for you, depending on

00:15:14.680 --> 00:15:16.630
your operating system, whether it's Mac

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:18.670
or Windows. If I type in Cloud in here,

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:20.510
then that will open up Cloud Code for

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:22.870
us, and uh it will ask us if we trust

00:15:22.880 --> 00:15:24.350
this folder. So, you can see I'm

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:26.510
accessing my root user folder in here,

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:27.630
and I'll say, "Yes, I trust this

00:15:27.640 --> 00:15:29.550
folder." And it will just show that same

00:15:29.560 --> 00:15:31.070
Cloud Code interface. So, right off the

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:33.070
bat, you can see it's not as intuitive

00:15:33.080 --> 00:15:35.070
that you can actually just select that

00:15:35.080 --> 00:15:37.470
my workspace empty folder that you were

00:15:37.480 --> 00:15:39.030
viewing earlier, and you need to learn

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:41.150
some terminal commands yourself in order

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:43.350
to navigate to that workspace folder if

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:45.310
you want to start using the uh terminal

00:15:45.320 --> 00:15:46.910
view effectively. So, I'll show you some

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:48.470
of the benefits of the terminal view in

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:50.470
just a bit, but uh just a note that

00:15:50.480 --> 00:15:52.110
whenever I need to access the terminal

00:15:52.120 --> 00:15:54.390
view, I actually just go to my IDE and

00:15:54.400 --> 00:15:56.190
access the terminal from here. So, from

00:15:56.200 --> 00:15:58.230
your side, you can just go on view and

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:00.150
find the terminal option in here, and it

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:02.190
will just open your terminal in one of

00:16:02.200 --> 00:16:04.310
these windows. For me, it's here on the

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:05.950
right sidebar. So, whenever I use the

00:16:05.960 --> 00:16:07.590
terminal, I just click this button at

00:16:07.600 --> 00:16:09.070
least on my side, and it will show up in

00:16:09.080 --> 00:16:10.430
here. And you can see there's already

00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:11.950
like this conversation that I was having

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:13.830
with it, so let's just open up like a

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:15.110
new session in there. And from this

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:16.830
terminal, the great thing about it is

00:16:16.840 --> 00:16:18.950
that it is already starting on my

00:16:18.960 --> 00:16:20.870
workspace, which is this one that I

00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:22.550
linked to it. And if I type in Cloud in

00:16:22.560 --> 00:16:24.790
here, then that will open Cloud Code

00:16:24.800 --> 00:16:26.710
operating from within this folder

00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:28.510
already. So, let me just maximize this

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:30.510
so you can see better. And in this

00:16:30.520 --> 00:16:32.470
terminal user interface view, it's going

00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:34.030
to be the same thing. Let's say I want

00:16:34.040 --> 00:16:37.070
to create a text file that says Robo

00:16:37.080 --> 00:16:39.110
Crew, then you can just type that

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:41.030
similar to how you would type in chat.

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:42.510
And you can see here that it is

00:16:42.520 --> 00:16:44.310
tinkering, it writes the output, and

00:16:44.320 --> 00:16:46.710
it's uh naturally a bit more verbose

00:16:46.720 --> 00:16:48.190
when it comes to the terminal view,

00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:50.190
where it shows you exactly what it does.

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:52.670
And the reason why that is is this view

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:54.310
is actually the original surface for

00:16:54.320 --> 00:16:55.829
Cloud Code because it was meant for

00:16:55.839 --> 00:16:57.310
developers, right? And so, you can see

00:16:57.320 --> 00:16:59.910
it created that Robo Crew.text for us,

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:02.070
but when we ask it to open it for us,

00:17:02.080 --> 00:17:04.470
then what it will basically do is just

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:07.230
open it in VS Code or whatever is your

00:17:07.240 --> 00:17:09.110
default for this text file. So, there

00:17:09.120 --> 00:17:10.910
you can see it opened my text file in

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:13.150
here, which is not ideal in my case

00:17:13.160 --> 00:17:15.270
because uh I didn't want to have like a

00:17:15.280 --> 00:17:17.350
third sort of application open on my

00:17:17.360 --> 00:17:19.150
laptop. Ideally, I just wanted like a

00:17:19.160 --> 00:17:21.230
text file open in my VS Code. So, that's

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:23.030
sort of one of the drawbacks when using

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:24.590
the terminal view because you don't

00:17:24.600 --> 00:17:26.670
really have visibility of your files.

00:17:26.680 --> 00:17:28.590
But, sometimes I do go in here just to

00:17:28.600 --> 00:17:30.550
test out new features that the Anthropic

00:17:30.560 --> 00:17:32.390
team is launching because a lot of

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:34.350
times, and it's less often now, but

00:17:34.360 --> 00:17:36.070
especially when Cloud Code was still

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:37.790
pretty new, a lot of the new features

00:17:37.800 --> 00:17:39.630
that they were having, like for example,

00:17:39.640 --> 00:17:41.870
this Ultra Code feature, first shipped

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:43.870
in the terminal view. And so, if you

00:17:43.880 --> 00:17:46.630
wanted to try out new tools and

00:17:46.640 --> 00:17:48.430
techniques, and if you're sort of on the

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:50.070
bleeding edge already of using Cloud

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:52.670
Code, then having at least that basic

00:17:52.680 --> 00:17:53.990
knowledge of how to use the terminal

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:56.590
view is going to be useful for you. Now,

00:17:56.600 --> 00:17:58.550
if I type in {slash}, there are actually

00:17:58.560 --> 00:18:00.150
other nifty commands in here, like for

00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:02.350
example, {slash} insights that would be

00:18:02.360 --> 00:18:04.070
useful for you as well, which we will

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:06.110
tackle in a more advanced lesson. And

00:18:06.120 --> 00:18:07.990
those sort of commands actually only

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:09.590
exist, at least right now, in the

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:11.350
terminal view, or at least they don't

00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:13.710
work as well when you use it in the VS

00:18:13.720 --> 00:18:15.590
Code extension or the Cloud desktop app.

00:18:15.600 --> 00:18:17.830
And so, in a nutshell, terminal view is

00:18:17.840 --> 00:18:19.430
the most intimidating, sure, but it has

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:21.470
the most features. But, at least for the

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:23.470
majority of people, I actually don't

00:18:23.480 --> 00:18:25.470
advise you to use the terminal view in

00:18:25.480 --> 00:18:27.270
your day-to-day because at least in the

00:18:27.280 --> 00:18:29.590
VS Code extension with this sort of

00:18:29.600 --> 00:18:31.710
session capability, then it is number

00:18:31.720 --> 00:18:33.550
one, a bit more familiar, number two,

00:18:33.560 --> 00:18:36.030
much more flexible, and number three, it

00:18:36.040 --> 00:18:37.950
is much more integrated where if you

00:18:37.960 --> 00:18:39.950
need to open files, need to open images,

00:18:39.960 --> 00:18:42.190
you'll be able to do it from just within

00:18:42.200 --> 00:18:44.030
this application. The desktop app is

00:18:44.040 --> 00:18:45.790
probably the least intimidating, but

